<!--
  THE DIVERGENCE WINDOWS UPDATE FEED.

  This file is the source of truth. Publishing means copying it to divergence-site, which serves it
  at https://divergenceapp.com/appcast-windows.xml : the URL compiled into R7 as
  kUpdateFeedUrlCompiled, and the URL every install that ever receives a build will ask forever.

  DO NOT HAND-EDIT ITEMS INTO IT. Items are written by make-appcast.ps1, which signs the enclosure
  with the escrowed Ed25519 key and then verifies that signature against the public key COMPILED
  INTO R7.exe before writing anything. A hand-added item skips both. The result of that is not a
  broken feed somebody notices; it is a feed the client silently rejects or silently ignores, whose
  only symptom is that no update ever installs.

  Note also that an XML comment may not contain two consecutive hyphens, which is why this one has
  none. A comment written with them does not warn anybody: it makes the whole feed unparseable, and
  the client's report for an unparseable feed is the same "no updates" as for an empty one.

  IT IS NO LONGER EMPTY. On 2026 09 07 the escrowed key was brought out, 1.0.301.0 (412edf6) was
  signed, published and verified, and the key was overwritten and deleted from the build machine in
  the same session. That was the first update ever delivered to Windows automatically; every build
  before it was handed to a tester by hand.

  The reasoning for the empty state is kept, because it governs any future gap between releases: an
  empty channel is a valid appcast and a useful one. WinSparkle reports no updates available, which
  is TRUE, and it keeps the feed's reachability testable separately from whether anything has been
  published. A placeholder item would instead be a promise pointing at a file that does not exist.

  DO NOT DELETE THE ENCLOSURE'S RELEASE. It lives on a divergence releases tag prefixed "win", in a
  repository shared with the Mac whose tags are v1.5.x. That prefix is what stops a Windows
  enclosure being swept up by somebody tidying "the old 1.0 releases". Deleting a release deletes
  its assets, and this feed would go on pointing at them, on every installed copy, forever.
-->
